Arora web browser

Arora is a cross platform web browser built using Qt and WebEngine
(QtWebKit is being ported away — see ChangeLog, "Qt 6 port").

Building:
Arora uses the qmake build system.  If you already have Qt 6 on your system building is as easy as running the command 'qmake' and then 'make' or 'nmake' on Windows.
For more detailed information see https://github.com/Arora/arora/wiki/BeginnerStepByStepInstructions

Optional adblock engine: the built-in C++ matcher is the default.  A
Brave adblock-rust backed engine can be enabled instead — build the
FFI static library with `cargo build --release` in src/adblock/rust
(needs a user-local Rust toolchain), then run `qmake CONFIG+=adblock_rust`
and rebuild.  `./arora --adblock-rust-smoke` compares both engines on a
fixed probe set.

Performance: `./arora --perf-smoke` runs headless (with
QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen) and reports cold-start, first-window,
tab-open, large-history-load, menu-open and profile permission-sweep
timings.  `./arora --profile-startup` (or ARORA_PROFILE_STARTUP=1 on a
normal launch) prints a millisecond timeline of the startup stages to
stderr.

Appearance: Arora follows the desktop light/dark preference (Qt 6.5+
color-scheme reporting).  On desktops that do not report a scheme —
or to force a look — set ARORA_COLOR_SCHEME=dark or light.

Icons: the toolbar/menu chrome resolves freedesktop icon names through
the desktop theme by default; the "Icons:" selector on the General
preferences page can instead pin a bundled Adwaita, Breeze or Tabler
set (each ships light + dark variants, applied live).  The original
2009 artwork remains the final fallback for names a set does not cover.

Search: the address bar doubles as a search box (omnibox) — text that
is not an address goes to the default search engine (DuckDuckGo unless
you pick another), while urls,
localhost/host:port and dotted names navigate directly.  Its dropdown
can show live suggestions above history matches, but only for engines
explicitly opted in (search button menu or the dedicated Search page
in Settings, which also picks the default engine); nothing is sent to
a suggest endpoint otherwise.  The dedicated search box next to the
address bar can be a full text field or a compact engine button
(Search settings page — the same preference the "Show dedicated
search box" checkbox on the General page toggles).  Button mode is
the default; Ctrl+K opens its engine menu with a search prompt, or
selects the field's text in field mode.  The Search page can also
assign separate engines to private/Tor windows and to image searches
(the image context menu's "Search Image" entry).

Extensions: Arora can run Chrome Manifest-V3 extensions through Qt
WebEngine's (tech preview) extension manager — the Extensions page in
the preferences dialog loads unpacked folders or installs .zip packages,
with a warning for chrome.* APIs Qt does not serve.  Plain user scripts
(any *.js file in the "userscripts" data directory) are injected into
every page at document-ready.

Tor: File → "New Tor Window" (or `arora --tor`) opens a separate Arora
process whose tabs all route through a managed Tor daemon — the web
engine's proxy is process-global, so Tor cannot be a per-tab feature.
Tor windows run a dedicated off-the-record profile (nothing persisted),
arm a fail-closed proxy until bootstrap completes, harden the surface
(no extensions, WebChannel bridges, autofill or search suggestions) and
carry a violet accent, "Tor" badge and "(Tor)" title marker.  Needs a
`tor` binary: system tor, the ARORA_TOR_BINARY env var, or the bundled
Expert Bundle via BuildProcess/fetch-tor.sh.  `./arora --tor-smoke` and
`./arora --tor-window-smoke` verify the daemon layer and the end-to-end
window routing headlessly.

Privacy: the "Connections & Storage" group on the Privacy preferences
page carries the hardening toggles, all defaulting to the private
choice.  Plain-http page loads are upgraded to https where a secure
endpoint plausibly exists (loopback, LAN addresses and .onion are
skipped; a site whose https fails is allowed over http for the rest
of the session — the error page says so).  The "Referer header"
selector controls what other parties see: "Trimmed" (the default)
sends cross-site requests the destination's own origin — nothing
about the page you came from, navigations and subresources alike —
and same-site requests just the origin, never the full path or
query; "Strict" sends nothing to other sites; "Never" withholds the
header everywhere.  Pages that deliberately withhold their referer
(noreferrer links, stricter site policies, insecure downgrades)
always stay silent, and a site redirecting you to a different origin
reveals at most the origin you came from — the one leg the engine
recomputes past our control.  WebRTC traffic is confined to
proxied/public interfaces
so voice/video sites cannot discover local or real IP addresses —
verified headlessly: ./arora --webrtc-smoke enumerates a real
RTCPeerConnection's ICE candidates and asserts none carry an IP
(--webrtc-off-smoke is the unprotected control).
Third-party cookies are rejected (per-site exceptions still apply),
and an opt-in "Clear browsing data when Arora exits" wipes history,
cookies, caches and site storage at quit — Chromium's on-disk trees
are removed at the next start, never under a running browser.  An
opt-in secure-DNS setting controls DNS-over-HTTPS: off (system
resolver), Chromium's automatic upgrade for resolvers on its
known-provider list, or a custom DoH endpoint (default
https://cloudflare-dns.com/dns-query) either with system-DNS
fallback or strict — strict fails resolution rather than leaking a
lookup to plain DNS when the endpoint is unreachable.  Honest bound:
with secure DNS off or in automatic mode the browser's lookups go to
the system resolver and are visible to the local network — only a
strict custom endpoint or the Tor window (whose SOCKS5 route tunnels
DNS too) hides them, and a system VPN/DoH resolver is the whole-OS
answer.  TLS
connections advertise only forward-secret AEAD cipher suites: the
RSA key-exchange and CBC suites are stripped from the ClientHello
(a toggle, on by default — a site that only supports the removed
suites will not load; takes effect after a restart).  The opt-in
"Fingerprint Resistance"
group reports UTC as the timezone to every site (JavaScript Date and
Intl reads) and a generic "en-US, en" language list instead of the
configured one; the Tor window's profile always normalizes both.
Honest bounds: an observer can still
see destination IPs, connection timing and the usual browser
fingerprinting surface — the Tor window is the answer for hiding
traffic endpoints, and QtWebEngine cannot reach Tor-Browser-level
fingerprint resistance.  CNAME-cloaked trackers (first-party aliases
that resolve to a tracking service) are not uncloaked: that needs
DNS-response inspection and QtWebEngine exposes no DNS-response hook
to the adblock engine.  Page-requested tracking uploads —
navigator.sendBeacon beacons, <a ping> link audits and CSP violation
reports — are dropped at the request layer (a "Block tracking pings
and beacons" toggle, on by default; CSP still enforces, only its
telemetry reports are suppressed, so sites lose the telemetry and
users lose the tracking channel).  `./arora --ping-smoke` proves
all three vectors against a loopback fixture.

Zero telemetry: a clean launch opens no unsolicited outbound
connections.  Chromium's background channels (field-trial/variations
fetches, component updater, domain-reliability reports, metrics,
sync, first-run pings) are disabled at startup.  The seeded adblock
filter lists (EasyList, EasyPrivacy, uBlock filters) stay dormant
until consent: first launch asks once, and subscribing via an
abp: link, ticking a list in the "Preset Filter Lists" catalog,
pressing "Update Subscription", or enabling the blocker
grants it.  There is no update checker or phone-home code.  Search
suggestions stay opt-in per engine (SEC11).  `./arora
--telemetry-smoke` watches a clean launch for 30s through a loopback
capture proxy plus a /proc socket scan and fails on any outbound
attempt.

The shield button at the left end of the address bar (beside the
site icon) opens a per-site privacy panel:
the page's security state, a per-site cookie exception (allow, block
or session-only) with the count of stored cookies, a content-blocking
toggle that whitelists the site (the icon greys while it is exempt),
a per-site JavaScript rule (site default, always allow, always block)
with the page's current script state, an "Allow pop-ups on this site"
toggle, the remembered permission
grants for the origin with revoke controls, and a clear-site-data
action.  The shield icon carries a small badge while JavaScript is
blocked on the current page.

A content-blocker button at the right end of the address bar shows a
per-page blocked-request count (as an icon badge, and in its popup),
toggles content blocking on or off globally, and opens the AdBlock
settings dialog.  The icon greys while blocking is disabled or the
site is whitelisted.

"Block Popup Windows" (Privacy settings, on by default) is a real
pop-up blocker: window.open calls that request a window shape are
refused inside the browser rather than merely suppressed when they
lack a user gesture, so click-triggered pop-ups are covered too.
While a page has refused pop-ups a small indicator appears at the
right end of the address bar with a count badge; its menu offers
each captured destination for "Open once", an "Always allow pop-ups"
exception for the site, and a shortcut to the settings.  Exceptions
apply to the site showing the pop-up (a rule for example.com covers
its subdomains), persist between sessions, and can also be managed
from the site panel or the Privacy page's remembered-permissions
list.  Tab-shaped opens (target=_blank links) are unaffected — they
follow the "open target-blank links in" preference.

A "Security Level" selector at the top of the Privacy settings page
offers three Mullvad-style tiers.  Standard (the default) is the
current behavior.  Safer blocks external scripts, workers and service
workers on pages loaded over insecure http and makes media
click-to-play — inline scripts on http pages can still run, and pages
served from this machine (localhost) keep their scripts because
Chromium treats them as secure contexts.  Safest disables JavaScript
on every site, which many websites will not survive.

A page whose scripts are blocked — by a per-site rule, by Safer on an
insecure http page, or by Safest — shows a slim bar across the top of
the page ("Scripts blocked on <host>") offering "Allow once"
(remembered until the browser closes) and "Always allow" (persisted;
on private-browsing pages it is session-scoped too).  An explicit
allow grant beats the tier in both directions, so a granted site runs
JavaScript even under Safest — including its external script fetches,
which the Safer http block would otherwise drop.  Rules are keyed by
host, a parent-domain rule covers subdomains, and the most specific
matching rule wins; the persistent rules are listed (and removable)
under Settings > Privacy > Site Permissions next to the other
per-origin grants.

Self-contained Linux bundle: `make bundle` (BuildProcess/bundle-linux.sh)
packs the binary, the whole Qt/WebEngine runtime (Qt libs, icu,
QtWebEngineProcess, webengine resources, plugins incl. qtwayland) and the
freedesktop metadata into dist/Arora-<ver>-linux-<arch>/ — a relocatable
directory that runs on a system with no Qt installed via ./arora.
`make check-bundle` verifies self-containment plus offscreen and native
Wayland startup in a bubblewrap sandbox with the dev Qt install hidden.
